 existing shader libraries must be rebuilt before launching this host. The regular
 build script detects the chain change. Its upstream image workflow needs Python
 3.12 or newer.
+
+## Render-pass backport (2026-11-01)
+
+Current candidate: the user reports an M3 iPad starting near 60 FPS and later
+running in the 40s. The supplied screenshot shows 48.98 FPS, GPU 18.89 ms,
+nominal thermal state and zero shaders compiling. It establishes GPU work
+above a 16.67 ms budget at that instant; it does not identify the cause of
+the change over time.
+
+This candidate backports Touch the Wild commits `1983d2d3cc44` and
+`2004eb433426`: guarded identical-target continuation, precise ordinary
+color-write accounting and compression-eligible feedback snapshots.
+Required copies remain; barrier/submission invalidation is unchanged.
+The [general mechanism](../../../docs/reference/rendering.md#render-pass-reuse-and-compression-without-removing-feedback-copies)
+includes the exact safety guards. The launcher exposes Memory transfer
+optimization, enabled by default. No game code, profile addresses or shader
+chain changes are needed; the original NXROM data identity is retained.
+
+Upstream reported exact before/after images for 24 BOTW replay comparisons,
+with 2-3 fewer passes and about 9-16 MiB/frame of estimated attachment
+traffic saved. These are upstream observations, not a benchmark of this
+backport or this iPad. The candidate includes the corresponding synthetic
+GPU regression tests. Physical-device sustained FPS and image acceptance
+remain open. Disable the optimization for a fresh-process comparison.
+
+The backport also corrects the existing shader-write classifier for writable
+storage-buffer addresses carried in uniforms (`NVN_RB_SBUFU`). These need the
+same encoder boundaries as directly bound storage buffers. The literal
+fragment-stage write contract fails before the correction and passes after;
+the CPU-only feedback test covers writable and read-only bindings as well as
+concurrent diagnostic sampling.
